<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
	<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
	<parent>
		<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
		<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
		<version>4.1.0</version>
		<relativePath/> <!-- lookup parent from repository -->
	</parent>
	<groupId>com.cartwise</groupId>
	<artifactId>cartwise-backend</artifactId>
	<version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
	<name>cartwise-backend</name>
	<description>CartWise backend service</description>

	<properties>
		<java.version>21</java.version>
		<!--
			jjwt is not managed by spring-boot-dependencies, so the version is pinned here.
			It is the one third-party version this project owns, which is why it gets a property
			rather than being repeated across the three artifacts below.
		-->
		<jjwt.version>0.12.3</jjwt.version>
		<!--
			JaCoCo is a build plugin, not a dependency, so spring-boot-dependencies does not manage
			it and the version is this project's to own. 0.8.13 is the floor for Java 21 class files
			(0.8.10 and earlier reject class file major version 65 outright); 0.8.15 is the current
			release and is pinned rather than floated for the same reason the container image is.
		-->
		<jacoco.version>0.8.15</jacoco.version>
		<!--
			Chapter 22. spring-boot-dependencies manages the Flyway *artifacts* (4.1.0 pins
			flyway-core 12.4.0), but it does not manage the flyway-maven-plugin, so the plugin
			version is this project's to own.

			It is pinned to the same 12.4.0 the parent resolves for the runtime, and that is not
			cosmetic. The plugin runs migrations from its own bundled copy of Flyway; if that copy
			were a different major version from the one inside the application, `mvn flyway:migrate`
			and the application's own startup migration would be two different engines writing the
			same `flyway_schema_history` table. Keeping them equal is what makes the command line
			and the boot path interchangeable.

			`mvn dependency:list` was used to read 12.4.0 off the resolved tree rather than the
			number being copied from documentation.

			The property is named `flyway-plugin.version` and NOT `flyway.plugin.version`, which is
			the obvious spelling and does not work. The plugin treats every Maven property under the
			`flyway.` prefix as one of its own configuration keys, so `flyway.plugin.version` is read
			as a Flyway setting and the build dies with

			    Unknown configuration property: flyway.plugin.version

			before it does anything else. Verified by hitting it. The hyphen takes the property out
			of that namespace; the three below stay in it deliberately, because they ARE Flyway keys.
		-->
		<flyway-plugin.version>12.4.0</flyway-plugin.version>

		<!--
			The dev database the flyway-maven-plugin talks to.

			These exist because the plugin is NOT a Spring component: it never reads
			application-dev.yml, so `mvn flyway:info` with no JDBC coordinates fails with
			"Unable to connect to the database. Configure the url, user and password!". The values
			duplicate application-dev.yml deliberately and are overridable on the command line
			(-Dflyway.url=...) so the same goals can be pointed at another database without editing
			the pom.

			Only the dev credentials are here, and only because they are the same worthless
			laptop-local pair already committed in application-dev.yml. A production migration is run
			by passing -Dflyway.url/-Dflyway.user/-Dflyway.password from the deployment platform's
			secret store, or by letting the application migrate itself on boot — which is the normal
			path and needs none of this.
		-->
		<flyway.url>jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/cartwise_dev</flyway.url>
		<flyway.user>cartwise</flyway.user>
		<flyway.password>cartwise</flyway.password>
	</properties>

	<!--
		Chapter 22 adds Flyway. The note that used to stand here said a migration tool "only earns
		its place once a deployed schema exists that must be evolved" — that is now true, so the
		dependency is below rather than in this list.

		Still not present, and why:
		  - No Liquibase                     -> Flyway is the choice. Two migration tools pointed at
		                                        one schema is not redundancy, it is two histories
		                                        that disagree.
		  - No OAuth2 client/resource-server -> social login is a later chapter; this one issues
		                                        its own tokens for its own users.
	-->
	<dependencies>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Brings in Spring Data JPA, Hibernate 7.4.1.Final and HikariCP. The artifact id is
			NOT renamed in Boot 4 the way the web starter was (web -> webmvc); verified against
			repo.maven.apache.org before adding.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Version is managed by spring-boot-dependencies (42.7.11 for Boot 4.1.0), so it is
			deliberately not pinned here — the parent and the driver stay in step on upgrade.
			Runtime scope: nothing compiles against the driver, it is looked up by JDBC URL.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
			<artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
			<scope>runtime</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			The whole starter, not just spring-security-crypto.

			Crypto alone would supply BCryptPasswordEncoder and nothing else — no SecurityFilterChain,
			no HttpSecurity, no filter chain to register a JWT filter into, which is most of what this
			chapter needs. The starter brings spring-security-config and spring-security-web as well,
			and crypto comes along inside it.

			Version is managed by the parent: Boot 4.1.0 pins Spring Security 7.1.0. That matters more
			than it sounds — Security 7 removed the chained DSL (`.csrf().disable()`), so only the
			lambda form in SecurityConfig compiles.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Chapter 22: versioned schema migrations.

			THE STARTER, NOT flyway-core. This is a Boot 4 change and it fails silently, which is the
			worst way for a change like this to fail.

			Every Flyway guide, and this chapter's own brief, says to add org.flywaydb:flyway-core and
			that Boot picks it up from the classpath. That was true through Boot 3, where
			FlywayAutoConfiguration lived inside spring-boot-autoconfigure and activated on
			@ConditionalOnClass(Flyway.class). Boot 4 split auto-configuration into per-technology
			modules, and the Flyway one moved to org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-flyway — which
			nothing drags in transitively.

			So flyway-core on its own gets you the library and no Spring wiring at all. Not a warning,
			not a "Flyway disabled" line: no Flyway output whatsoever, `spring.flyway.*` properties
			read and ignored, and then the first symptom is Hibernate refusing to start with

			    Schema validation: missing table [comparisons]

			which points at the entity mapping and says nothing about migrations. That is exactly what
			happened here on the first boot against the fresh database, and it is why this dependency
			is the starter: spring-boot-starter-flyway brings spring-boot-flyway (the
			auto-configuration) along with flyway-core, so the wiring cannot be missing while the
			library is present.

			With the starter in place, Boot orders Flyway BEFORE the EntityManagerFactory is created.
			That ordering is the whole reason `ddl-auto: validate` can work on an empty database:
			migrations build the schema, and only then does Hibernate compare the entities against it.

			Versions managed by spring-boot-dependencies (Boot 4.1.0 pins Flyway 12.4.0), so neither
			is pinned here — same reasoning as the PostgreSQL driver above.

			flyway-database-postgresql is separate and required. Flyway 10 split every database
			implementation out of the core jar, so the core knows no dialects by itself and fails with

			    Unsupported Database: PostgreSQL 17.11

			which reads like a version-support problem and is actually a missing-module problem. Most
			Flyway examples still show only flyway-core, from before the split.

			Runtime scope on the database module: nothing in src/main/java imports a Flyway class.
			Migrations are SQL files, and the only thing that "calls" Flyway is the auto-configuration.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
			<artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
			<scope>runtime</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			jjwt, split the way its authors intend: the API is compiled against, the implementation
			and the JSON binding are runtime-only. Nothing in this codebase should be able to import
			an io.jsonwebtoken.impl class by accident, and the scopes are what enforce that.

			jjwt-jackson pulls in Jackson 2 (com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind) for
			serialising claims. Spring Boot 4 uses Jackson 3 (tools.jackson) for HTTP bodies, so two
			Jackson generations end up on the classpath. They do not collide — the package names are
			entirely different — and each is used by exactly one thing: Jackson 3 for the API's JSON,
			Jackson 2 for the inside of a token. Worth knowing before it looks like an accident.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
			<artifactId>jjwt-api</artifactId>
			<version>${jjwt.version}</version>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
			<artifactId>jjwt-impl</artifactId>
			<version>${jjwt.version}</version>
			<scope>runtime</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
			<artifactId>jjwt-jackson</artifactId>
			<version>${jjwt.version}</version>
			<scope>runtime</scope>
		</dependency>

		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Test support for authentication. Not strictly required by this chapter's tests, but it is
			the artifact that makes an authenticated request testable without hand-building a token,
			and it belongs next to the starter it supports rather than being remembered later.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>

		<!--
			Chapter 21 adds tests. Three artifacts, all test-scoped, none of them versioned here
			because spring-boot-dependencies imports the Testcontainers BOM and pins them in step
			with the Boot version.

			spring-boot-testcontainers is the Spring-side glue: @ServiceConnection, which reads a
			started container's host/port/credentials and registers them as the DataSource before
			the context refreshes. Without it the alternative is @DynamicPropertySource and three
			lines of string-building per test class, which is the same thing written by hand and
			wrong more often.

			Why a container at all, rather than H2: application-dev.yml already refuses H2 on the
			grounds that a dialect-compatible fake is how PostgreSQL-specific bugs survive to
			production. A repository test run against H2 would assert that the *fake* behaves —
			the exact thing that is not worth asserting. Container tests run PostgreSQL 17.6, the
			deployed version.

			The cost is honest: these tests need a Docker daemon, so they are guarded (see
			com.cartwise.testsupport.RequiresDocker) and skip rather than fail where none exists.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
			<artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<!--
			Note the artifact ids: `testcontainers-junit-jupiter` and `testcontainers-postgresql`,
			NOT `junit-jupiter` and `postgresql`. Testcontainers 2.x renamed every module to carry
			the `testcontainers-` prefix, and Boot 4.1.0 imports testcontainers-bom 2.0.5. The 1.x
			coordinates that most tutorials still show resolve to nothing here, and the error Maven
			gives is "'dependencies.dependency.version' ... is missing" — which reads like a missing
			BOM rather than a wrong artifact id, and sends you looking in the wrong place.
		-->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
			<artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
			<artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
	</dependencies>

	<build>
		<plugins>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
				<artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
			</plugin>

			<!--
				Chapter 22. The plugin is NOT how migrations are applied in normal operation — the
				application runs them itself on boot, on every machine and every environment, which
				is the property that makes a deployment self-sufficient.

				What the plugin adds is the ability to ask questions without starting an application:
				`flyway:info` prints the applied/pending state of a database, `flyway:validate`
				re-checks committed migrations against their recorded checksums, and `flyway:repair`
				fixes a history row after a failed migration. Those are the operations you want when
				the application is the thing that will not start.

				Note the artifact id: `flyway-maven-plugin`. The name `maven-flyway-plugin` reads more
				naturally and does not exist — Maven's own plugins are `maven-*-plugin` and
				third-party ones are `*-maven-plugin`, and getting it backwards produces a plugin
				resolution error rather than an obviously wrong name.

				No <executions>. Binding `migrate` to a lifecycle phase would mean `mvn test` or
				`mvn package` silently writes to a database, which is a build that has side effects
				on something outside the build. These goals are run deliberately or not at all.

				`cleanDisabled` is Flyway's default and is restated here as a decision rather than
				an inheritance: `flyway:clean` drops every object in the schema, and this chapter
				exists precisely to stop schema destruction from being one command away. Dropping the
				dev database is still possible, it just has to be spelled out in psql where it cannot
				be reached by a mistyped Maven goal.
			-->
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
				<artifactId>flyway-maven-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>${flyway-plugin.version}</version>
				<configuration>
					<url>${flyway.url}</url>
					<user>${flyway.user}</user>
					<password>${flyway.password}</password>
					<cleanDisabled>true</cleanDisabled>
					<locations>
						<location>classpath:db/migration</location>
					</locations>
				</configuration>
				<!--
					The plugin resolves `classpath:` locations against ITS OWN classpath, not the
					project's, so without these two dependencies it reports "Skipping filesystem
					location" / finds zero migrations even though the files are plainly there. The
					driver is needed for the same reason: the plugin runs outside the application, so
					nothing else puts a PostgreSQL driver in front of it.

					Versions are not pinned; they are inherited from the same dependencyManagement
					that pins the runtime artifacts, so the plugin and the application cannot drift.
				-->
				<dependencies>
					<dependency>
						<groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
						<artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
						<version>${flyway-plugin.version}</version>
					</dependency>
					<dependency>
						<groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
						<artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
						<version>42.7.11</version>
					</dependency>
				</dependencies>
			</plugin>

			<!--
				The test JVM runs in UTC, and this is not a style preference.

				The PostgreSQL JDBC driver sends the client JVM's default time zone to the server as
				the connection's `TimeZone` parameter. This machine's JVM reports `Asia/Calcutta` —
				the legacy alias the JDK still uses for India Standard Time — and the Debian-based
				postgres:17.6 image does not recognise it: `pg_timezone_names` has `Asia/Kolkata` and
				no `Asia/Calcutta`, so every connection is refused with

				    FATAL: invalid value for parameter "TimeZone": "Asia/Calcutta"

				and every container-backed test dies at "Unable to determine Dialect without JDBC
				metadata", which names nothing to do with the actual cause.

				Verified rather than assumed: `SELECT count(*) FROM pg_timezone_names WHERE
				name='Asia/Calcutta'` returns 0 inside that image and 1 for 'Asia/Kolkata'. The
				PostgreSQL 17.11 installed natively on this machine accepts both, which is why the
				pre-chapter baseline run against the dev database passed and only the container path
				fails — a difference between two PostgreSQL builds, not between two versions.

				Fixing it by naming Asia/Kolkata would work here and leave the next machine to
				rediscover it. UTC is the setting that makes the suite independent of where it runs,
				which is the property actually wanted: CartWise stores `Instant` everywhere and
				`timestamp with time zone` in every table, so nothing under test has an opinion about
				the local zone.

				@{argLine} is late evaluation of the property JaCoCo's prepare-agent sets. Writing a
				plain <argLine> here would replace it and silently produce an empty coverage report —
				the agent would never be attached, and the build would stay green while measuring
				nothing.
			-->
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
				<configuration>
					<argLine>@{argLine} -Duser.timezone=UTC</argLine>
				</configuration>
			</plugin>

			<!--
				JaCoCo measures which bytecode branches the tests actually executed.

				`prepare-agent` binds to initialize and puts a Java agent on the surefire JVM's
				command line; `report` turns the resulting jacoco.exec into HTML at
				target/site/jacoco/index.html.

				`report` is bound to `test`, not to JaCoCo's own default of `verify`. The default is
				the right one for a project whose pipeline runs `mvn verify`; this one is driven by
				`mvn clean test`, and a report goal bound to a phase that never executes produces no
				report at all while the build still goes green — the worst of both, because it looks
				like coverage is being measured. The binding follows the command that is actually run.

				Deliberately NOT bound: the `check` goal, which fails the build below a coverage
				threshold. A threshold is only meaningful once there is a measured baseline to set
				it from — wiring it before the first report would either pick a number out of the
				air or fail every build until it happened to be met.

				The exclusions are types with no behaviour to cover. Counting a record's generated
				accessors as "uncovered lines" does not describe a risk, it just moves the
				percentage, and a coverage number that can be moved without writing a test is a
				number that stops meaning anything.
			-->
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
				<artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>${jacoco.version}</version>
				<configuration>
					<excludes>
						<exclude>com/cartwise/CartwiseBackendApplication.class</exclude>
						<exclude>com/cartwise/**/dto/**</exclude>
						<exclude>com/cartwise/config/*Properties.class</exclude>
						<exclude>com/cartwise/**/package-info.class</exclude>
					</excludes>
				</configuration>
				<executions>
					<execution>
						<id>jacoco-prepare-agent</id>
						<goals>
							<goal>prepare-agent</goal>
						</goals>
					</execution>
					<execution>
						<id>jacoco-report</id>
						<phase>test</phase>
						<goals>
							<goal>report</goal>
						</goals>
					</execution>
				</executions>
			</plugin>
		</plugins>
	</build>

</project>
